                <pb facs="0363" n="253" sameAs="frus:frus1928v03/pg253"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.5048/6: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Minister in
                        Liberia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Francis</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-09-01T13:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">September 1,
                                1928—1 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>29. Your 34, August 30, 6 p.m. King’s telegrams to the Department and the
                    Associated Press have received full publicity in the press and I have
                    telegraphed to him direct on the subject. The extreme nature of Buell’s charges
                    would in any event have tended to offset their effectiveness while the speech
                    delivered by Thomas Jesse Jones at Williamstown on August 29 commending the
                    Firestone concession and the American loan and the Department’s Liberian policy,
                    the extensive comments made by the Department to the press regarding Buell’s
                    speech, and President King’s statement should dispose of Buell’s charges and
                    clear the air of misunderstandings.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Clark</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
